(Audit Evidence and the Concept of Materiality) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf
T*
(The purpose of an audit is to obtain reasonable assurance that the financial statements) Tj T*
(as a whole are free from material misstatement, whether caused by fraud or error. The) Tj T*
(auditor expresses an opinion on whether the statements are prepared, in all material) Tj T*
(respects, in accordance with the applicable financial reporting framework.) Tj T*
T*
(Materiality is a matter of professional judgment. Information is material if omitting,) Tj T*
(misstating or obscuring it could reasonably be expected to influence the decisions that) Tj T*
(users make on the basis of the financial statements. The auditor determines a) Tj T*
(materiality threshold when planning the engagement, and that threshold is often) Tj T*
(expressed as a percentage of revenue, total assets or profit before tax.) Tj T*
T*
(Sufficient appropriate audit evidence supports the auditor's opinion. Appropriateness) Tj T*
(concerns the relevance and reliability of the evidence; sufficiency concerns its) Tj T*
(quantity. Evidence obtained directly by the auditor is generally more reliable than) Tj T*
(evidence obtained indirectly. Documentary evidence originating from external sources is) Tj T*
(more reliable than evidence obtained internally.) Tj T*
T*
(Substantive procedures are designed to detect material misstatements at the assertion) Tj T*
(level. They include tests of details and analytical procedures. Tests of details may) Tj T*
(involve inspecting records, recalculating amounts, confirming balances with third) Tj T*
(parties, or observing the client's procedures. Analytical procedures evaluate financial) Tj T*
(information by studying plausible relationships among both financial and non-financial) Tj T*
(data.) Tj T*
T*
(The auditor must also evaluate the risk of management override of controls. Because) Tj T*
(management is in a unique position to perpetrate fraud, the auditor designs procedures) Tj T*
(to address this risk regardless of the assessment of other risks. Such procedures may) Tj T*
(include testing journal entries, reviewing accounting estimates for bias, and evaluating) Tj T*
(the business rationale of significant transactions.) Tj T*
T*
(Going concern is the assumption that the entity will continue in operation for the) Tj T*
(foreseeable future. If the auditor concludes that a material uncertainty exists, the) Tj T*
(auditor must consider how the matter is disclosed in the financial statements and) Tj T*
(whether the disclosure is adequate.) Tj T*
T*
(Inventory often represents a significant balance. The auditor attends the physical count) Tj T*
(to observe the client's procedures, inspect the condition of goods, and identify) Tj T*
(obsolete or damaged items. Cutoff testing ensures that transactions are recorded in the) Tj T*
